# How We Will Solve the Problem

The system will follow a centralized event-processing architecture.

## Step 1 — Collect Data

Data will originate from two primary sources:

### Linux Agent

The Linux agent will collect operating-system information such as CPU, memory, disk, processes, and network information.

### Technology SDKs

Application SDKs will allow applications to send errors, logs, stack traces, requests, and performance information to the monitoring platform.

## Step 2 — Send Events to the Collector

Collected events will be sent to the Event Collector API using HTTP/REST APIs.

The collector will be responsible for:

- Receiving events
- Authenticating requests
- Validating incoming data
- Applying basic rate limits
- Forwarding events for processing

## Step 3 — Queue Events

An event queue can be introduced between collection and processing.

The queue will provide:

- Buffering
- Asynchronous processing
- Backpressure handling
- Better reliability when event volume increases

Redis and BullMQ are possible technologies for this layer.

## Step 4 — Process Events

The Processing Engine will process incoming events.

The processing pipeline will:

Receive Event
     ↓
Parse
     ↓
Validate
     ↓
Filter
     ↓
Categorize
     ↓
Detect Issues
     ↓
Store

## Step 5 — Store Events

Processed events will be stored in MongoDB.

The database will contain information related to:

- Logs
- Errors
- Metrics
- Traces
- System events
- Service information
- Timestamps
- Environment information

## Step 6 — Expose Data Through REST APIs

The backend will provide REST APIs that allow the frontend to:

- Fetch events
- Search logs
- Filter events
- View individual errors
- View system information
- Retrieve analytics
- Access monitoring information

## Step 7 — Display Information

The frontend dashboard will consume these REST APIs and present the collected information in a developer-friendly interface.

The developer will therefore have a centralized view of:

Application
    +
Services
    +
Linux System
    +
Network Events
    +
Errors
    +
Logs
    +
Metrics
        ↓
Central Monitoring Dashboard

## Technology Stack
### Backend
- Node.js
- Express.js
- REST API
### Database
- MongoDB
### Queue
- Redis
- BullMQ (optional)
### Frontend
- React.js
- EJS / HTML / HTMX may be evaluated depending on the final frontend architecture
### Monitoring
- Linux Agent
- Technology-specific SDKs
### Deployment
- Vercel / Cloudflare for backend where appropriate
- Netlify for frontend

The final deployment architecture will depend on the requirements of the monitoring agents and long-running backend services.

## Expected Outcome

The final system should allow a developer to identify and investigate application and infrastructure problems from a centralized platform instead of manually searching through different services and log files.

For example:

Application Error
      ↓
SDK captures error
      ↓
Event Collector
      ↓
Processing Engine
      ↓
Categorized Event
      ↓
MongoDB
      ↓
Dashboard
      ↓
Developer identifies
service + error + timestamp
+ system context

This will make debugging, monitoring, and system health analysis faster and more organized.
